Memory Model Rules (8):
--------------------------------
<1> 4 Rules about what reordering is allowed

<2> 2 Rules about stores across strands.
    -> if one proc sees a store all procs should see it too
    -> multiple stores to the same address are seen in the same order by all processors

<2> 2 Rules about Locks
    -> Total Lock order (irrespective of lock address)
    -> Locked instructions have store semantics. So question being  Lock Load reordering allowed?
       Answer no!! Meaning Locked instructions come with an implicit post membar #StoreLoad barrier
       <Hint: Volatile stores can be implemented using a locked write>


------------------- DETAILS ------------------------------------------------
TLO - CC (Total Lock Order - Causal Consistency)
1. Reads are not reordered with other reads (on one processor).

2. Writes are not reordered with other writes (on one processor).

3. Writes are not reordered with older reads (on one processor).

4. Reads may be reordered with older writes to different locations but
not with older writes to the same locations (on one processor later
reads can move upwards).

5. In a multiprocessor system, memory ordering obeys causality (mem-
ory ordering respects transitive visibility; P1: W(x,1), P2: R(x),
W(y,1), P3: R(y), R(x)).
[Kutty] That is if one processor sees your write rest assured all other processors have seen it too.


6. In a multiprocessor system, writes to the same location have a total
order (e.g., P3 and P4 both read x twice, if P3 sees 1, 2 P4 can not
see 2, 1).
[Kutty] All processors agree on sequence of writes to the same location

7. In a multiprocessor system, locked instructions have a total order.

8. Reads and writes are not reordered with locked instructions (e.g., func-
tion as barrier).
